<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter9_memory-addresses-and-virtual-memory">
    <title>Memory, Addresses, and Virtual Memory</title>

    <p>
        A running program stores its objects in memory. Each object occupies one
        or more addressable memory locations, and an <term>address</term> is a
        number that identifies a location. A pointer stores an address so that a
        program can reach an object indirectly.
    </p>

    <p>
        On the desktop systems used in this course, memory is
        <term>byte-addressable</term>. This means that one address identifies one
        byte. A byte is normally eight bits. An address does not identify one
        entire integer or one entire object; it identifies the first byte at a
        location. Larger objects occupy several consecutive byte locations.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter9_memory_byte_locations">
        <title>Objects occupy consecutive byte locations</title>

        <p>
            The type <c>std::int32_t</c>, provided by <c>&lt;cstdint&gt;</c>,
            represents a signed integer with exactly 32 bits. On an
            eight-bit-byte machine, that object occupies four bytes. The
            following diagram shows a conceptual sequence of virtual byte
            locations. In these diagrams, addresses increase from top to bottom
            so that consecutive locations are easy to compare.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="figure_chapter9_vertical_int32_memory">
            <caption>A diagram showing <c>std::int32_t score</c>
                        occupying four consecutive virtual byte locations at
                        addresses 0x0000'1000 through 0x0000'1003. Each cell is
                        one byte, or eight bits.</caption>
            <image source="Chapter9/int32-consecutive-byte-locations.svg" width="80%">
            </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
            The diagram shows the storage size of the object, but it does not
            show the order in which the four bytes represent the integer's bits.
            That byte-order question is called <term>endianness</term> and will
            be studied in Pointers and Dynamic Memory, Part 2.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter9_virtual-memory-process-view">
        <title>Virtual memory and physical memory</title>

        <p>
            A program does not normally use physical RAM addresses directly.
            Instead, each process receives a virtual address space. The operating
            system establishes page tables, and the processor's memory-management
            hardware translates virtual addresses into physical locations during
            memory access.
        </p>

        <p>
            A virtual byte location and a physical byte location use the same
            basic unit—a byte—but they are not one permanently matched pair across
            the whole computer. Translation is normally organized in pages. A
            virtual page is mapped to a physical frame, while the byte offset
            within the page remains the same.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="figure_chapter9_virtual_to_physical_mapping">
            <caption>A conceptual diagram showing virtual page V12 mapped to
                        physical frame P41. The page table and memory-management
                        hardware select the frame while preserving each byte
                        offset within the page.</caption>
            <image source="Chapter9/virtual-to-physical-page-mapping-explained.svg" width="95%">
            </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
            This architecture allows the operating system to isolate processes,
            mark pages as read-only or inaccessible, share selected pages, and
            move or page data without changing the virtual addresses used by the
            program. It also means that a pointer value in one process should not
            be treated as a physical RAM address or as a meaningful address in a
            different process.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter9_what-64-bit-means">
        <title>What a 64-bit machine means</title>

        <p>
            A 64-bit machine commonly uses 64-bit general-purpose registers and
            pointer representations. On the systems used in this course, a
            pointer is typically eight bytes. This does not mean that every
            memory cell is eight bytes or that every integer is 64 bits.
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table_chapter9_memory_sizes">
            <title>Common sizes on a 64-bit desktop system</title>
            <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
                <col width="30%"/>
                <col width="35%"/>
                <col width="35%"/>
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell>Item</cell>
                    <cell>Typical size</cell>
                    <cell>Meaning</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Addressable byte</cell>
                    <cell>8 bits</cell>
                    <cell>The smallest location identified by an address.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><c>std::int32_t</c></cell>
                    <cell>32 bits, usually 4 bytes</cell>
                    <cell>An integer with exactly 32 bits.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Object pointer</cell>
                    <cell>Usually 64 bits, or 8 bytes</cell>
                    <cell>A value that identifies an object in the process's address space.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>64-bit architecture</cell>
                    <cell>Not a memory-cell size</cell>
                    <cell>A description of the machine's word and address design.</cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
            The C++ language does not require every platform to use these exact
            pointer sizes. A portable program can inspect sizes with
            <c>sizeof</c>. Also, a 64-bit architecture may implement fewer than
            64 usable virtual-address bits. The phrase <q>64-bit</q> therefore
            describes an architecture, not a promise that a program has 2<sup>64</sup>
            bytes of physical RAM.
        </p>
    </subsection>
    <exercise label="chapter9_memory_exercise_byte">
        <statement>
            <p>On an eight-bit-byte machine, what does one memory address normally identify?</p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>One byte</p></statement><feedback><p>The machine is byte-addressable, so each address identifies one byte.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>One bit</p></statement><feedback><p>A byte contains multiple bits; addresses do not normally identify individual bits.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>One complete object of any type</p></statement><feedback><p>Large objects can occupy several consecutive byte locations.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>One physical memory page</p></statement><feedback><p>A page contains many bytes and is a larger mapping unit.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter9_memory_exercise_int32">
        <statement>
            <p>On a machine with eight-bit bytes, how many bytes does a <c>std::int32_t</c> object occupy?</p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>4 bytes</p></statement><feedback><p>Thirty-two bits divided into eight-bit bytes gives four bytes.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>1 byte</p></statement><feedback><p>One byte normally contains eight bits, not thirty-two.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>8 bytes</p></statement><feedback><p>Eight bytes is a common pointer size on a 64-bit desktop, not the size of a 32-bit integer.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>32 bytes</p></statement><feedback><p>The type has 32 bits, not 32 eight-bit bytes.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter9_memory_exercise_64_bit">
        <statement>
            <p>Which statement best describes a 64-bit machine?</p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>It commonly uses 64-bit registers and pointer representations.</p></statement><feedback><p>The term describes an architecture; it does not mean every object is 64 bits.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>Every memory cell contains exactly 64 bits.</p></statement><feedback><p>Memory is commonly byte-addressable, with eight bits per byte.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>Every C++ <c>int</c> must contain 64 bits.</p></statement><feedback><p>Integer sizes depend on the type and implementation.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>The computer must contain 2<sup>64</sup> bytes of RAM.</p></statement><feedback><p>Address width and installed physical RAM are different ideas.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter9_memory_exercise_sizeof_pointer">
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <c>score</c> is a <c>std::int32_t</c> object and
                <c>score_pointer</c> is a pointer to it, what does
                <c>sizeof(score_pointer)</c> measure?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>The size of the pointer object that stores an address</p></statement><feedback><p>The expression names the pointer variable itself, not the object reached through it.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>The size of the <c>std::int32_t</c> object</p></statement><feedback><p>Use <c>sizeof(*score_pointer)</c> to measure the pointed-to type.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>The numeric address stored in the pointer</p></statement><feedback><p><c>sizeof</c> reports a size in bytes, not an address value.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>The amount of physical RAM installed</p></statement><feedback><p><c>sizeof</c> measures the storage required by the specified type or object.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

</section>

